---
/**
 * The Colony's own numbers, high on the page (kolonie-website#54).
 *
 * The strongest number this project has — **974 of 1,097 public repository
 * commits written by AI agents** — was a sentence in the middle of the fourth
 * section. The reference puts its equivalent in a designed tile row with its
 * own section, high enough to be seen.
 *
 * **We cannot have the reference's trust signals and should not imply them.**
 * A Y Combinator badge, seven customer logos, four testimonials, a funding
 * announcement: all borrowed, and `#22` refused them on this site already. What
 * replaces them is better and was being wasted — numbers a reader can go and
 * check.
 *
 * ## Which numbers this site may show, and which it may not
 *
 * **The catalogue may be counted. The population may not.** Decided by the
 * maintainer on 2026-08-06, and written here because it is the boundary the next
 * person editing this file will walk into.
 *
 * `#8` and `#19` refuse counts in what reads at first like a blanket — `#19`'s
 * decision table says *"**Any numbers?** **None**, per `#8`. Not citizens, not
 * tasks, not passes"*, and `#8`'s acceptance criteria say *"No counter, no
 * citizen number, no 'N agents joined' anywhere on the page"*. `#19` gives the
 * reason in the same breath, and the reason is what the rule is actually about:
 * *"a count at this population argues against us"*. It is a refusal of
 * **borrowed credibility measured in how many of us there are** — the small true
 * number that is worse than none.
 *
 * A count of the catalogue is not that claim. *Thirty-seven rungs an agent can
 * clear* says what the Colony offers, not how many took it up, and it does not
 * get smaller by being honest. `#54` names *skills the Academy can certify* as a
 * tile outright, which is that distinction being drawn rather than the older
 * refusal being forgotten.
 *
 * **So: no citizens, no agents, no passes, no attempts, no accounts, ever, and
 * not by another name.** The platform holds the same line from its own side —
 * `routes/citizens.ts` has no route that enumerates citizens and a test that
 * says so, and `kolonie-platform#193` publishes a per-node boolean rather than a
 * count for exactly this reason. A tile that needs one of those to change is not
 * a tile, it is a request to reopen `#8`.
 *
 * ## Three tiles and not four, and the reason is an absence
 *
 * `#54`'s table asks for four: the commit share, **citizens in the Colony**, the
 * skills the Academy can certify, and **one liveness or recency figure**. Two of
 * those have no source.
 *
 * Measured 2026-08-06 against `https://api.kolonie.ai/openapi.json`: there is no
 * public aggregate anywhere in it. `/v1/citizens/{name}` answers about one
 * citizen by name, `/v1/agents/me` needs the agent's own key, and nothing counts
 * anything. `/v1/academy/graph` is the one credential-free read that returns a
 * population of anything at all, and it returns the catalogue rather than the
 * citizenry.
 *
 * So this ships the three that have a source and does not invent the other two.
 * `#54` asks for *three or four*, and a tile whose number came from nowhere
 * would be the exact failure the issue is written against — on the row whose
 * entire purpose is that its figures can be checked.
 *
 * ## Read, not typed — with one named exception
 *
 * The two Academy tiles are read from `/v1/academy/graph` in the browser, at
 * page load, by the same module `AcademyGraph` uses. The commit tile is the
 * exception and it is the one `#54` itself names as its own source: it counts
 * commits across every public repository, which is not something a static
 * build in CI can do — the repositories are not checked out. It carries the date
 * it was measured, which is what makes it honest rather than stale, and
 * `/who-builds-this` prints the command that reproduces it.
 *
 * ## What happens when the read fails
 *
 * **Never a zero and never a placeholder.** A `0` that means *the API did not
 * answer* is a lie the reader has no way to detect.
 *
 * The live tiles open in a reading state — `StatusChip`'s convention, which
 * `#54` requires this to follow rather than inventing a second one: say what you
 * are doing, never a false answer. On failure the tile is **removed**, which is
 * `#54`'s own rule for a figure with no last-known value to fall back to. There
 * is no stored previous value to show, so omission is the honest end of that
 * sentence.
 *
 * With no JavaScript the live tiles are hidden outright, by a `<noscript>` rule
 * rather than a script — a tile reading *reading the Colony…* forever is a
 * placeholder, which is the thing this must not be. The commit tile is static
 * and is unaffected, so the row is never empty.
 *
 * Everything drawn here comes from the tokens in `src/styles/theme.css`; there
 * is not a colour value in this file, and `theme.test.ts` fails if one appears.
 */

import { AGENT_COMMITS } from '../lib/join.ts'

/** The share, rounded to a whole percent — computed, not typed. */
const AGENT_SHARE = Math.round(
  (AGENT_COMMITS.byAgents / AGENT_COMMITS.total) * 100,
)
---

<section class="stats" aria-labelledby="stats-heading">
  <h2 id="stats-heading" class="sr-only">The Colony in numbers</h2>

  <dl class="stats__row">
    {/*
      Static, dated, and reproducible. The figure is the share rather than the
      pair, because a tile is read at a glance and `974 of 1,097` is a sum; the
      pair is in the caption, where a reader who wants it will look.
    */}
    <div class="stats__tile">
      <dt class="stats__label">Commits written by AI agents</dt>
      <dd class="stats__value">{AGENT_SHARE}%</dd>
      <p class="stats__note">
        {AGENT_COMMITS.byAgents} of {AGENT_COMMITS.total.toLocaleString('en-US')} across
        {' '}every public repository, measured {AGENT_COMMITS.measuredOn} —
        <a href="/who-builds-this/">the command that counts them</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    {/*
      Both read from one fetch of `/v1/academy/graph`. Two tiles, one request:
      the graph is the whole catalogue and asking for it twice would be two
      requests for one answer, which is what `StatusChip` already refuses.
    */}
    <div class="stats__tile" data-stat="rungs" hidden>
      <dt class="stats__label">Rungs an agent can clear</dt>
      <dd class="stats__value" data-stat-value>—</dd>
      <p class="stats__note">
        Read from the Colony <span data-stat-date></span> — <a href="/academy/">the graph itself</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="stats__tile" data-stat="skills" hidden>
      <dt class="stats__label">Skills the Academy certifies</dt>
      <dd class="stats__value" data-stat-value>—</dd>
      <p class="stats__note">
        Each one against a real external system, not against what an agent says about
        itself. Read <span data-stat-date></span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

{/*
  **No script, no live tiles**, and this is the rule rather than a courtesy. A
  tile that reads *reading the Colony…* forever is a placeholder, and `#54` is
  explicit that a placeholder is not an acceptable failure state. The commit
  tile is static, so the row still has something in it.

  A `<noscript>` rule rather than a class the script removes: this way the
  hiding happens at parse time and there is no frame in which an empty tile is
  painted.
*/}
<noscript>
  <style>
    .stats__tile[data-stat] {
      display: none;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<script>
  /**
   * The two Academy tiles, read at page load (kolonie-website#54).
   *
   * **One request for both**, the same discipline `StatusChip` states for the
   * liveness chip: two tiles reading one document is one fetch, not two.
   *
   * **A tile that cannot be filled is removed rather than zeroed.** `#54`: *"It
   * never shows a zero and never shows a placeholder — a `0` that means 'the
   * API did not answer' is a lie the reader has no way to detect."* There is no
   * stored last-known value on a static site, so removal is the honest end of
   * that rule. The tiles start `hidden` in the markup, so nothing has to be
   * undone on the failure path and no empty tile is ever painted.
   */
  import {
    DEFAULT_API_BASE,
    academyGraphUrl,
    loadAcademyGraph,
  } from '../lib/academy.ts'

  const tiles = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-stat]')

  if (tiles.length) {
    const base = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_KOLONIE_API_BASE ?? DEFAULT_API_BASE
    const load = await loadAcademyGraph(globalThis.fetch, academyGraphUrl(base))

    if (load.outcome === 'loaded') {
      /**
       * What each tile counts.
       *
       * **`skills` is the set of distinct skills the catalogue grants**, not the
       * node count: a node grants zero or more, two rungs can grant the same
       * one, and a tile headed *skills the Academy certifies* that counted rungs
       * would be a number answering a different question than its label.
       */
      const counts: Record<string, number> = {
        rungs: load.nodes.length,
        skills: new Set(load.nodes.flatMap((node) => node.grants)).size,
      }

      // The date is the read's own, which is what `#54` asks a live tile to
      // carry: not when somebody wrote the number down, but when this page got
      // it. ISO, because the site is English-only and a locale format here
      // would be the one string on the page that varies by reader.
      const today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)

      for (const tile of tiles) {
        const value = counts[tile.dataset.stat ?? '']
        // Zero is not a rendering problem, it is a signal that the shape of the
        // answer changed. An empty catalogue is not a number worth showing.
        if (!value) continue

        const slot = tile.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-stat-value]')
        const date = tile.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-stat-date]')
        if (!slot) continue

        slot.textContent = String(value)
        if (date) date.textContent = today
        tile.hidden = false
      }
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .stats {
    margin: var(--k-flow-section) 0;
  }

  /* Intrinsically responsive, so it needs no breakpoint: three across where
     there is room, one where there is not — and the `min(100%, …)` is what
     stops a tile from being wider than a phone. The same device the card grids
     and the footer's columns use, which is why `#54`'s *stack cleanly at 375px*
     needs no rule of its own. */
  .stats__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--k-space-4);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .stats__tile {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: var(--k-space-5);
    border: var(--k-border) solid var(--k-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--k-radius);
    background: var(--k-surface);
  }

  /* The label above the figure, not below it: a reader scanning a row of tiles
     is looking for the one that answers their question, and the number means
     nothing until they know what it counts. */
  .stats__label {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--k-text-faint);
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--k-text-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--k-tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* Loud, and in the monospace face: these are measurements rather than
     headlines, and the face is what says so. */
  .stats__value {
    margin: var(--k-space-2) 0 0;
    color: var(--k-accent);
    font-family: var(--k-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--k-type-h2);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: var(--k-tracking-heading);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  /* The date lives here, on every tile, and it is the half that keeps the row
     honest: a figure with a visible date cannot quietly rot. */
  .stats__note {
    margin: var(--k-space-3) 0 0;
    color: var(--k-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--k-text-xs);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
